# AI Coding Standards & Module Creation Guide

This document provides comprehensive guidelines for AI agents creating or modifying modules in this codebase.

---

## 1. Module Directory Structure

Every module follows this standard structure:

```
internal/modules/{module}/
├── {module}mdl/
│   ├── {module}.go          # Database entity model
│   ├── {module}_req.go      # Request models (API input)
│   ├── {module}_rsp.go      # Response models (API output)
│   └── {module}_dto.go      # Data transformation objects
├── {module}svc/
│   ├── {module}svc.go       # Service definition, lifecycle, public API
│   ├── {module}svc_ops.go   # Ops thread: CREATE, UPDATE, DELETE handlers
│   ├── {module}svc_query.go # Query thread: GET, LIST handlers
│   └── {module}_cache.go    # Cache implementation (if needed)
└── {module}hdlr/
    └── {module}hdlr.go      # HTTP handlers
```

**Rules:**
- ALL models live in `{module}mdl/` (entities, requests, responses, DTOs)
- NO global `internal/models/` directory - each module owns its models
- Business logic lives in `{module}svc/`
- Transport logic (HTTP) lives in `{module}hdlr/`
- Database access is performed ONLY by the Service layer, NEVER by Handlers
- Cross-module model references use imports (e.g., `usermdl.User`)

---

## 2. Architecture: Lock-Free Channel Pattern (CRITICAL)

The application uses a specific concurrency pattern to avoid mutex contention in hot paths.

**Rules:**
- Use write-first caching (DB write → cache update)
- Lock-free caching using channels
- `sync.Mutex` must NOT be used for caching

### Pattern Overview

Every Service (`*Svc`) runs two dedicated goroutines:
1. **`opsRunThread`** (Writer): Handles all `CREATE`, `UPDATE`, `DELETE` operations
2. **`queryRunThread`** (Reader): Handles all `GET`, `LIST` operations. Owns the in-memory cache

### Data Flow

```
Handler → Channel → Service Goroutine → DB/Cache → Channel → Handler
```

